:ruby
  PageHeader = import("/components/PageHeader.haml")
  DocsPage = import("/components/DocsPage.haml")
  DocsSection = import("/components/DocsSection.haml")

%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Docs",
    title: "Router Plugin",
    summary: "RouterPlugin is an optional, framework-agnostic plugin that discovers filesystem routes and generates a virtual router module."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Configure The Router" }
    :markdown
      Add `RouterPlugin` to the build context and import its virtual module.

      ```ruby
      router_plugin = Klenod::Build::Plugins::RouterPlugin.new(
        specifier: "virtual:router",
        pages_dir: "routes"
      )

      context = Klenod::Build::Context.new(
        source_dir: "src",
        plugins: [
          *Klenod::Build::Context.default_plugins,
          router_plugin
        ]
      )

      router = context.entry("virtual:router").exports::Default
      match = router.match("/blog/hello")
      ```

      In an application config, either append `router_plugin` to `Context.default_plugins` or provide a full ordered plugin list.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Supported Files And Segments" }
    :markdown
      The router discovers `+page.rb`, `+page.haml`, `+route.rb`, `+layout.rb`, `+layout.haml`, `+error.rb`, `+error.haml`, `+not-found.rb`, and `+not-found.haml`.

      Supported segment forms include:

      - `[id]` dynamic segments.
      - `[...slug]` catch-all segments.
      - `[[...slug]]` optional catch-all segments.
      - `(marketing)` route groups.
      - `@modal` parallel route slots.
      - `(.)photo`, `(..)profile`, and `(...)login` intercepted route segments.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Match Results" }
    :markdown
      A route match exposes the selected modules and structural metadata:

      ```ruby
      match.page
      match.handler
      match.layouts
      match.slots
      match.params
      match.route
      ```

      Generated router modules use `lazy_import` so matching a route can load only the selected page, handler, layouts, and slots.

      When no page route matches, the router returns the closest `not-found` module for that URL path. Frameworks can use the closest `error` module when rendering raises.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Framework Boundary" }
    :markdown
      RouterPlugin does not decide request dispatch or rendering policy. Hybrid directories can contain both a page and `+route.rb`; the router exposes both and the consuming framework decides which one to use for a request.

      The example configures `Example::Framework::Route` as the route-handler base class. It makes `Response` and `localized_path` available directly inside `+route.rb` files.

      The example application keeps its document shell in `/root.haml`, separately from filesystem route layouts. `RouterApp` composes the matched page and layouts, discovers their route-scoped assets, and passes the ordered asset references only to the root component. The root Haml template renders `html`, `head`, `body`, stylesheet links, module scripts, and the composed route content.

      Route content remains lazy until the document is prepared, so a `provide_context` boundary in the root can wrap route components. Preparation evaluates every component and lazy child once under the active request and rendering contexts. A route can render `%title` to replace the root template's default document title; preparation collects all title declarations before output starts, and the last declaration wins.

      After preparation, HTML serialization can stream the normalized document without JavaScript, string-range replacement, or component rerendering. The final title is emitted at the root title's location, while later declarations are omitted from their original locations.

